---
slug: sign-in/email-code
method: post
layout: ''
searchable: false
metadata: {
    page_name: "Email Me a Sign-In Code - Submit",
}
---

{%- liquid
  if context.current_user
    redirect_to '/dashboard'
    break
  endif

  comment
    A post without a valid authenticity token is not refused: it arrives with an empty
    session. The form page sets this flag, so its absence means the token was missing or wrong.
  endcomment
  if context.session.sign_in_form != 'on'
    redirect_to '/sign-in/email-code?error=session'
    break
  endif

  assign email = context.params.email | default: '' | strip | downcase
  assign valid = true
  if email.size < 6 or email.size > 254 or email contains ' '
    assign valid = false
  endif
  unless email contains '@'
    assign valid = false
  endunless
  unless valid
    redirect_to '/sign-in/email-code?error=invalid'
    break
  endunless

  comment
    Codes are stored as an HMAC keyed with SIGN_IN_HMAC_KEY, which a migration creates.
    Without the key no code can be checked, so none is sent.
  endcomment
  if context.constants.SIGN_IN_HMAC_KEY == blank
    redirect_to '/sign-in/email-code?error=unavailable'
    break
  endif

  comment
    5 requests per address and 20 per IP address in 15 minutes. Every request counts the
    same whether or not the address has an account, so the limit tells nobody which do.
  endcomment
  function allowed = 'account/rate_limit/allow', action: 'sign_in_code', key: email, max_per_key: 5, max_per_ip: 20
  unless allowed
    redirect_to '/sign-in/email-code?error=too_many'
    break
  endunless

  function return_to = 'account/safe_return_to', path: context.params.return_to
  function ip = 'account/rate_limit/client_ip'
  function account = 'account/sign_in/allowed_user', email: email

  comment
    Six random digits. random_string gives letters and digits, so keep the digits and
    top up until there are six.
  endcomment
  assign code = ''
  for i in (1..10)
    assign digits = 32 | random_string | replace_regex: '[^0-9]', ''
    assign code = code | append: digits
    if code.size >= 6
      break
    endif
  endfor
  assign code = code | slice: 0, 6

  function code_hash = 'account/sign_in/code_hash', email: email, code: code

  assign now_unix = 'now' | date: '%s' | plus: 0
  assign expires_at = now_unix | plus: 900 | date: '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ'
  assign status = 'miss'
  if account.id != blank
    assign status = 'pending'
  endif

  comment
    One row and the same answer for every address. Only an address with an account gets
    an email. The row keeps a hash of the code, never the code.
  endcomment
  graphql saved = 'account/sign_in/create_code_request', email: email, code_hash: code_hash, expires_at: expires_at, status: status, ip: ip, return_to: return_to

  if status == 'pending' and saved.record_create.id != blank
    assign mail = {}
    assign mail['to'] = email
    assign mail['code'] = code
    assign mail['minutes'] = 15
    graphql sent = 'account/sign_in/send_sign_in_code', data: mail
  endif

  comment
    Keep the table small: requests live 15 minutes, so anything older than a day can go.
  endcomment
  assign day_ago = now_unix | minus: 86400 | date: '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ'
  graphql cleared = 'account/sign_in/delete_old_requests', table: 'sign_in_code', before: day_ago

  comment
    Remember the address so the code page can fill it in. It is not trusted there: the
    code page checks whatever address it is sent.
  endcomment
  graphql remembered = 'account/create_session', name: 'sign_in_code_email', value: email

  redirect_to '/sign-in/code?sent=1'
-%}
